      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Method"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 3
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Make the filling"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "orderedList",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Cook {{onion}} finely diced onion in a little oil over medium heat for 5 minutes. Add {{beef-mince}} of mince and brown well. Add {{plain-flour}} of flour, stir for 1 minute, then add {{stock-beef}} of beef stock and {{worcestershire-sauce}} of Worcestershire sauce. Simmer for 20 minutes until thick. Season well and cool."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 3
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Make the pastry"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "orderedList",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Rub {{unsalted-butter}} of cold butter into {{plain-flour}} of flour and {{sea-salt-fine}} of salt until like coarse crumbs. Mix in enough of the beaten {{eggs}} to bring the dough together. Divide two-thirds for the base, one-third for the lid. Chill 20 minutes."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 3
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Assemble and bake"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "orderedList",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Heat oven to 200 degrees C. Line a buttered pie dish with the larger pastry piece. Fill with the cooled mince. Top with the lid, seal the edges, trim, and brush with egg. Cut a steam hole in the centre. Bake for 30 to 35 minutes until deep golden."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 3
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Make the mash and liquor"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "orderedList",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Boil {{potato}} of potato until tender. Drain and mash with {{unsalted-butter}} of butter and {{whole-milk}} of warm milk until smooth. Season well."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Melt {{unsalted-butter}} of butter, add {{plain-flour}} of flour, stir for 1 minute, then whisk in {{stock-chicken}} of chicken stock gradually. Simmer 5 minutes. Remove from the heat and blend in {{parsley-flat}} of parsley leaves with a hand blender until the sauce is vivid green. Season and pour hot over the mash and pie."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "troubleshooter",
        "attrs": {
          "rows": [
            {
              "symptom": "Pastry base is soggy",
              "cause": "Filling not cooled before adding to the pastry, or pie not baked hot enough",
              "fix": "Always use a completely cooled filling. The steam from hot filling softens the raw pastry before it has a chance to set."
            },
            {
              "symptom": "Liquor is pale rather than vivid green",
              "cause": "Parsley not added at the end, or overcooked after blending",
              "fix": "Add the parsley off the heat and blend immediately. Do not boil after blending or the green will turn drab."
            },
            {
              "symptom": "Mince is dry and grainy",
              "cause": "Overcooked or not enough stock used",
              "fix": "The filling should be saucy and thick but not dry. Add a splash more stock if it looks too stiff."
            },
            {
              "symptom": "Pie lid cracks or falls in",
              "cause": "Pastry too thick or not rested enough before baking",
              "fix": "Roll the lid to about 3 mm thickness. Seal the edges firmly and cut a steam hole so the pastry does not push up then collapse."
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Where this dish lives"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Pie and mash is the defining dish of London's East End food culture. Pie-and-mash shops opened in London from the 1840s onwards, serving cheap, filling food to working-class communities. The shops still exist: Manze's, Kelly's, and a handful of others. The original shops served jellied eels alongside the pie; the liquor was made from the eel-cooking water. Modern pie-and-mash shops use a stock-based parsley sauce. The combination has not changed in 150 years."
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  }
